! Steward Doctrine ~ the cabal that engineers its own inability to rule

''PROPOSED — unbuilt design intent.'' This meme names a steady bearing the build moves toward, not a shipped contract. It rests at the Synthesis register; the gates and ceremonies it names await the tiered-gate build (cross-check before any RULED language).

The Amorphous Dreams cabal founds the stack and maintains it — yet a founder who keeps repair authority slides toward ruler unless the design ''binds'' that slide structurally. This meme draws the binding. The cabal ''gardens the structure''; it never operates the output. ''Legitimacy flows from being BOUND, not from being trusted'' — the cabal engineers its own inability to act as one, then publishes that inability so any peer may audit it. The steward survives by giving up the very power a ruler hoards.

<<~ ahu #/steward-not-ruler>>

!! Steward, Not Ruler ~ the four-part binding

A maintainer body crosses into stewardship — never rulership — by carrying four bindings at once. Strip any one and the body re-grows the throne it meant to vacate. The mature commons-governance lineage witnesses each (Debian, Rust, Apache, Tezos, DNSSEC):

| # | binding | what it does | witnessed as |
|---|---|---|---|
| ① | ''constitution sits OUTSIDE unilateral reach'' | the founding rules amend only by a body WIDER than the cabal; anyone proposes | Debian's GR — a 3:1 developer supermajority amends the constitution, the project leader cannot |
| ② | ''authority delegated DOWNWARD + accountable'' | the steward gardens structure, never operates output; delegates carry their own scoped seats | Apache's PMC — the foundation charters, the project self-governs |
| ③ | ''signing key held k-of-n'' | the cabal STRUCTURALLY cannot act as one hand; the secret splits across diverse holders | DNSSEC root — a 3-of-7 threshold of geographically-separate key-holders |
| ④ | ''auditable + FORKABLE'' | the ultimate check rides exit, not voice; rhizomatic-by-construction stays forkable-by-construction | Rust editions / any git lineage — a stewardship that drifts gets forked away from |

The four compose: the constitution names what the cabal may not touch alone ①, the delegations push power away from the centre ②, the threshold forbids the centre from acting as one ③, and the fork stands ready when the first three fail ④. ''The cabal's legitimacy rests on the published proof of all four'', never on a plea to trust its intentions.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/legitimacy-from-binding>>

!! Legitimacy From Binding ~ the inversion

A ruler asks for trust and accumulates power against the day trust runs short. A steward does the inverse: it ''sheds'' the power to act unilaterally, then ''publishes the shedding'' as its credential. The move inverts the usual flow —

<<~moves ruler-legitimacy/asks-to-be-trusted -> steward-legitimacy/proves-it-cannot-betray on/published-binding if/k-of-n-and-forkable do/audit-the-structure-not-the-actor>>

So a peer never weighs //"do I trust Amorphous Dreams?"// — it weighs //"can I verify the threshold, the constitution's amendment-width, and the fork-exit?"// The check moves from a judgement about hidden intent to an audit of visible structure — the same shift the object-capability model makes from ambient trust to explicit edges ([[vessel-caps|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps]] integrity). A cabal that cannot structurally betray needs no trust to be safe.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/board-verdict>>

!! The Board Verdict ~ separate the planes

A single cap-tiered document that mixes the steward's writing hand with the public's reading eye fuses two planes that the architecture already cuts apart (the AUTHORITY ⊥ FLOW master cut). The board verdict keeps them severed:

* the ''steward SOURCE'' — a threshold-signed document the cabal authors k-of-n (binding ③), carrying the constitutional core, the delegation roster, and the amendment rule.
* projects to a ''verify-only public READ plane'' — every peer verifies the threshold signature and reads the result; no peer (and no single cabal member) writes it.

The READ plane carries the governance projection, never re-defines it — see [[oracle-governance|lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/oracle-governance]] for the read-plane's own law (this meme defers to it, never restates it). The shape rhymes the projection-nalu: a sovereign SOURCE → gated wave → verify-only SINK, `role = capability ≠ platform`. ''NOT one cap-tiered doc'' — two planes, one signing shore between them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/break-glass>>

!! Break-Glass ~ repair authority stays bounded

The steward keeps a repair hand — the power to sign the zone, re-seed a corrupted genesis, evict a captured key. That hand stays ''bounded on four edges'' so repair never quietly widens into rule. "Designation carries authority + fail loud" governs every edge:

* ''ceremonial / ephemeral'' — the holder's share stays inert outside the scripted ceremony, the way a DNSSEC key-holder's smartcard does nothing until the filmed signing ritual convenes it. The authority lives in the ceremony, not in the person.
* ''narrowly typed to repair'' — the repair hand signs the zone; it cannot change //who signs//. Re-keying the membership of the threshold itself demands a HIGHER threshold than any single repair.
* ''quorum-gated'' — no break-glass fires below the k-of-n floor; one captured holder turns nothing.
* ''time-delayed for anything constitutional'' — a change to the founding rules waits out a multi-period delay, the way Tezos requires a >80% super-majority sustained across multiple voting periods. The delay gives the wider body time to see, object, and fork.

The four edges keep break-glass a //repair// primitive, never a //governance// primitive — it restores the system to its own constitution, it never rewrites that constitution unilaterally. A repair hand that could change who-signs would carry the throne back in through the maintenance door.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/grounding>>

!! Grounding ~ the commons-governance lineage

Every binding above stands on a system that already runs it at scale; the doctrine invents no new governance primitive, it composes proven ones:

* ''Debian Constitution'' — amendment by 3:1 developer super-majority; the project leader holds influence, never unilateral rule (binding ①).
* ''Rust editions / Apache PMC'' — delegated, accountable, downward-flowing stewardship of structure (binding ②).
* ''DNSSEC root KSK'' — 3-of-7 threshold of diverse key-holders, ceremonial signing, the smartcard inert between rituals (bindings ③ + break-glass).
* ''Tezos on-chain amendment'' — >80% multi-period super-majority for protocol change; time-delay as a constitutional brace (break-glass time-delay).
* ''Git / fork-by-construction'' — exit as the ultimate check; a rhizome forks where it disagrees (binding ④).

The convergence reads strong: every durable open-governance body that survived capture did so by binding its own founders, not by trusting them. The Amorphous Dreams cabal inherits that lineage rather than improvising against it.

<<~/ahu>>

